 Or another example if your position adjustment assumes it can do 1 additional buy with 3x the original stake amount then `custom_stake_amount` should return 25% of proposed stake amount and leave 75% for possible later position adjustments.
 
+#### Limiting exposure to a single underlying currency
+
+If several of your pairs are priced against, or backed by, the same underlying currency (for example a handful of `BTC`-margined futures, or several coins all quoted in the same stablecoin), a move in that one currency can hit several open trades at once - even though each individual trade looked fine in isolation.
+
+The underlying currency of a pair is its settlement currency for margined/futures pairs (the part after the `:` in the pair, e.g. `BTC` for `ETH/USD:BTC`), or its quote currency otherwise (e.g. `USDT` for `BTC/USDT` and `BTC/USDT:USDT`).
+
+To cap the combined stake committed to any single underlying currency across all simultaneously open trades, set `max_underlying_currency_exposure` to a ratio of your tradable capital ([as defined above](#tradable-balance)).
+
+```json
+"max_underlying_currency_exposure": 0.3,
+```
+
+With the configuration above, no more than 30% of the tradable capital may be committed at once to trades that share an underlying currency. When opening (or adding to, via position adjustment) a trade would push that combined exposure above the configured limit, the stake amount for that trade is reduced to fit under the limit, the same way stake amounts are adjusted for other sizing limits (e.g. [minimum trade stake](#minimum-trade-stake)) - if even the minimum tradable stake doesn't fit under the limit, the trade is skipped instead. This applies identically in live/dry-run and in backtesting.
+
+!!! Note
+    This setting is disabled (no cap) by default.
+
